From 50eca85eb0dd37b95be9817cfe1486ee928a7173 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 20:14:22 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 144/144] puppet: a skeleton's explicit matrices are its rest
 pose

A puppet whose skeleton (MDLS v2+) carries its flagged block of explicit matrices drew its parts apart under its
animation layers. Workshop 3071833748's fringe hung over the lower face as a dark blob beside a bald forehead, and
3102766419's hair and pink pieces floated off a bald head. The loader skipped that block as redundant inverse binds and
took the bone matrices for both the bind and the rest pose. But in these files the bone matrices sit on the parts as
the puppet's image lays them out (apart), the explicit matrices are where the parts go (together), and the animations
are keyed at the explicit pose, up to 960 px from the bone matrices. An additive layer adds its sample minus the rest,
so each one moved the parts only that fraction of the way: a third, in 3071833748.

Wallpaper Engine 2.8.797 reads a bone's rest matrix through one getter (GH 0x140249ec0; the IK targets' GH 0x140249ef0):
the explicit matrix when the file has them, else the bone's own. Its animator (GH 0x14024d9a0) takes that for the rest
pose the layers mix from and for the pose with no layer, while the inverse binds come from the bone matrices
(GH 0x14024b710). So the core now keeps both per bone: `local`, the bind the inverse binds are built from, and `rest`,
the explicit matrix when there is one; `restPose` (was `bindPose`) decomposes the rest, and an IK target's matrix is its
explicit one. A puppet whose rest isn't its bind is skinned even without an animation, and the autosize canvas measures
its rest pose too.

Tested by liascape's patches/lwe/tests/puppet_test.cpp, which compiles this file in and poses 3071833748's own skeleton
values: at frame 0 its two additive layers leave a fringe vertex where the rest pose puts it, its bone's rest world
minus its bind world (560.2, -652.5) px from the mesh, and at frame 30 they turn it about bones 1 and 2 as worked out
by hand; a skeleton without the block rests at its bone matrices and skins to the mesh. Red on c18727c (the fringe a
third of the way: (190.5, -221.9) px).

Origin: ours
Why: Workshop scenes 3071833748 and 3102766419 (and 3013489778's other puppet) drew their characters apart
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5, effort high <noreply@anthropic.com>
